8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541055 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541055 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541055 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541055 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c5e30b2851a8268c5193c2f87e3c6ab77645db4d165d83150fb504ba06ef5c2.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $H(6,0).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541055 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01d2d95e-a1f1-5169-b942-8df45709b352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541055 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541060 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541060 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327800" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541060 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541060 #edb/ref 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541060 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0d95a82d-3596-5013-a69c-59119b644955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541060 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4 \\\\ \\frac{x+4}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2 \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3\\sqrt{3}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{27}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541062 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/13/13036c467cbe4fdd245b12c481de08ac1eb14bbfcc50653309a913ab1e9d8463.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11028c29-bebb-5855-afbb-0a0cead7b8d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541062 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(4,-2),$ $a=7$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541086 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541086 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541086 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327167" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541086 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541086 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a4/a41c728f9a82b563203fa0eb6944617948cf12c048be2553b803b06820d0b896.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-3,4)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $6,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,4).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541086 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bd0d286-b955-5de9-a7f0-4b2c20b02f7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541086 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-3,4),$ $a=2$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541087 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541087 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cc/cc9e723494b5d592b97ff1b4795eb1bc0ca7be0c4608591792eb2ac5515a9811.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bd7c7cd-dbab-5060-9d8d-5c720fc231fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541087 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=1$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/6e/6eeff954ea6f159a3bdc781361da094319bcf06a5365d12e894228bb172a16e6.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541118 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541118 #edb/ref 1069 "q-327807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541118 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541118 #edb/ref 1071 "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0a23b4a-b8a9-57f6-be01-ea66f5dec94e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541118 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{50} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\sqrt{7}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{7}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(3\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{63} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{50}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{63}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541125 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541125 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541125 #edb/ref 1069 "q-326265" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541125 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541125 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bb/bbfc3c75c4df