\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541141 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541141 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79283" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541141 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541141 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ea/ea74c15f3d02478a7486f967b7d2eebc780d1b5bd53ac21a81934b6438531f6d.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $5,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541141 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db10a605-9f1c-5d1d-923c-41dc3d49c705" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541141 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541144 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541144 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541144 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4b/4bccea09ccf18444f5b9505a8a753a9065ea97a5dc622208c281926c17cd5de1.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate the ellipse shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "de318b1b-b80f-58da-a7ea-29f3d4789691" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541144 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,3),$ $a=3$ and $b=6.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541145 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541145 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541145 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541145 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/00/008a79d8328b5b58cdc68a39a921125a1efa7e0b46a663b1ede9dc6773fb48e8.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541145 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df871080-1b98-5262-9947-842390c2ab3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541145 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/82/82436a7f248ed63683a3164d8c372433abb6f919567defa05700ebf88c50308f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541147 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186540973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541147 #edb/ref 1069 "q-15801" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541147 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541147 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7435811-f6b1-5cc6-8105-467790c4f32b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541147 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{7} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{7}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{49}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106401" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541152 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/41/410d6ec438e066487cd6de9d4bcbf429362a5a86d5893e7820a832d2fffb9bbd.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f6c896d6-3993-5114-bd3a-88e176c55230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186541152 #edb/ref 1074 "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/06/06e4a1a4d50943deff0b50eb510ca7ad4676ad6015a9503f7ce279f249078858.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
